From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] up: EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT, frame fence waits park the vi2 service thread (port of Lichii PR #1639 by DuskDarf1; off unless set)

Upstream: zortos293/Lichii PR #1639 "nx2: suspend fixed frame-fence waits in the guest kernel" by
DuskDarf1 (head dbfbd68761eb), open and unbuilt upstream on 13 Nov 2026. Ported by reading the PR diff.

Why: nx2:vi2's present runs on the sm server's service thread, a guest thread on guest core 3
(services.cpp RunOnGuestCoreProcess, InitializeServiceThread, priority 16). WaitForFrame blocks the
host thread of that core on a condition variable until the frame's fence: past the field 27-35 ms of
a 63 ms frame (measured by others). No other guest thread can run on core 3 meanwhile.

What (only with EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT=1):
- WaitForFrame's full waits (not the frames-ahead probe with unless_gpu_waits, not with
  EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS) clear one per-session KEvent, register a scoped fence action that
  signals it (on the GPU thread, as nx2:syncpt's slot events are signalled), and wait on it with
  KSynchronizationObject::Wait in 50 ms rounds, keeping the deadline (4 s or EDEN_TEST_FRAME_WAIT_MS)
  and the power-off check. Leaving the wait deregisters the action; our DeregisterGuestAction also
  joins one already running, so the next wait's Clear never races a late Signal.
- Not on a guest thread (a dummy KThread) or no event: the old host wait, counted.
- The session's destructor drops the action and closes the event (as upstream).
- One line per 600 waits: "EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT: last 600 frame waits ...": parked, average
  wait, kernel waits, 50 ms timeouts, terminations, signal-to-run time (the guest reschedule cost
  this adds to each present), and waits that kept the host wait. One line at the first parked wait.

Not taken from upstream: their nx2_fence_action.h as a file (the class is in nx2_gsv.h, so no
CMakeLists change) and their src/tests case (our build does not build the tests).
Not covered: WaitForFrameSlack / WaitForFrameCapped's own waits and WaitForFrameFront's wait for
the split front keep their host waits (their WaitForFrame calls are covered).

Unset (the default): one cached static read per frame wait; behaviour as T181. nx2_gsv.h is not a
translation id input and no name is added to TranslationSwitches: KEEPS THE STORE.

---
diff --git a/src/core/hle/service/nx2_gsv.h b/src/core/hle/service/nx2_gsv.h
index 33d99f9e88..8441e0a196 100644
--- a/src/core/hle/service/nx2_gsv.h
+++ b/src/core/hle/service/nx2_gsv.h
@@ -65,6 +65,7 @@
 #include "core/hle/kernel/k_hardware_timer.h"
 #include "core/hle/kernel/k_readable_event.h"
 #include "core/hle/kernel/k_session.h"
+#include "core/hle/kernel/k_synchronization_object.h"
 #include "core/hle/kernel/k_thread.h"
 #include "core/hle/kernel/k_transfer_memory.h"
 #include "core/hle/kernel/svc_results.h"
@@ -1895,6 +1896,31 @@ inline Nx2DecoderImageLayout Nx2CalculateDecoderImageLayout(const u32* preferenc
     return layout;
 }
 
+/// EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT (after Lichii PR #1639 by DuskDarf1, open and unbuilt upstream on
+/// 13 Nov 2026; their Nx2ScopedGuestFenceAction, nx2_fence_action.h): owns one guest-fence action
+/// until it ran or its waiter left. Our SyncpointManager::DeregisterGuestAction also waits for an
+/// action already running outside its lock, so after the destructor the action never runs.
+class Nx2ScopedGuestFenceAction final {
+public:
+    template <typename Callback>
+    Nx2ScopedGuestFenceAction(Tegra::Host1x::SyncpointManager& syncpoints_, u32 id_, u32 value,
+                              Callback&& callback)
+        : syncpoints{syncpoints_}, id{id_},
+          handle{syncpoints.RegisterGuestAction(id, value, std::forward<Callback>(callback))} {}
+
+    ~Nx2ScopedGuestFenceAction() {
+        syncpoints.DeregisterGuestAction(id, handle);
+    }
+
+    Nx2ScopedGuestFenceAction(const Nx2ScopedGuestFenceAction&) = delete;
+    Nx2ScopedGuestFenceAction& operator=(const Nx2ScopedGuestFenceAction&) = delete;
+
+private:
+    Tegra::Host1x::SyncpointManager& syncpoints;
+    const u32 id;
+    const Tegra::Host1x::SyncpointManager::ActionHandle handle;
+};
+
 // Placeholder for the Switch 2 display session (nn::vi2::sdk), handed out by gsv 305. Reply
 // shapes read from the SDK: TIPC 1 -> u32 < 0x400, TIPC 3 -> u32 < 8, TIPC 11/31(u32) and
 // 32(handle, u32) -> one handle at word 3 then the result. Handles returned here are events (a
@@ -1977,6 +2003,12 @@ public:
     }
 
     ~INx2Vi2Session() override {
+        // EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT (Lichii #1639): a service fiber can be dropped at shutdown
+        // without unwinding its stack. Remove or join the fence action before closing its event.
+        m_park_action.reset();
+        if (m_park_event != nullptr) {
+            m_context.CloseEvent(m_park_event);
+        }
         // The vsync callback may be running on the HostTiming thread: empty its event list under
         // its lock first (it holds the state, not this session), then close the events.
         {
@@ -2789,6 +2821,149 @@ private:
     u64 m_test_gave_up_total{};
     std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point m_test_wait_window{};
 
+    /// EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT=1 (port of Lichii PR #1639 by DuskDarf1, "nx2: suspend fixed
+    /// frame-fence waits in the guest kernel"; open and unbuilt upstream on 13 Nov 2026). Off
+    /// (unset, the default): one cached static read per wait, nothing else. This session's
+    /// requests run on the sm server's service thread (services.cpp: RunOnGuestCoreProcess,
+    /// guest core 3, priority 16). The plain wait blocks that core's host thread on a condition
+    /// variable, so no other guest thread runs on core 3 until the fence (27-35 ms a frame past
+    /// the field). Parked, the service thread waits on a session event in the guest kernel; the
+    /// fence action signals it from the GPU thread (as nx2:syncpt's slot events already are), and
+    /// core 3 runs other guest threads meanwhile. The present itself still returns at the fence:
+    /// the game thread that presents waits exactly as long as before.
+    static bool ParkWaitSwitch() {
+        static const bool on = Common::PeSwitch("EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT");
+        return on;
+    }
+
+    /// One reusable event per session; a failed allocation keeps the host wait for good.
+    bool EnsureParkEvent() {
+        if (m_park_event == nullptr && !m_park_event_tried) {
+            m_park_event_tried = true;
+            m_park_event = m_context.CreateEvent("nx2:vi2 frame fence wait");
+            if (m_park_event == nullptr) {
+                LOG_WARNING(Service, "EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT: no event for the frame wait; "
+                                     "this session keeps the host wait");
+            }
+        }
+        return m_park_event != nullptr;
+    }
+
+    /// Returns false (and waits not at all) when it cannot park: not on a guest thread, or no
+    /// event. Otherwise waits as WaitForFrame's host wait does (the fence, the deadline, power
+    /// off) and returns true; the caller then reads the fence as before.
+    bool ParkFrameWait(u32 fence_id, u32 fence_value, std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point start,
+                       std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point deadline) {
+        auto& kernel = system.Kernel();
+        Kernel::KThread* const self = Kernel::GetCurrentThreadPointer(kernel);
+        if (self == nullptr || self->IsDummyThread()) {
+            // A host thread (a dummy KThread): a kernel wait would block it all the same.
+            ++m_park.host_thread;
+            NoteParkWaits();
+            return false;
+        }
+        if (!EnsureParkEvent()) {
+            ++m_park.no_event;
+            NoteParkWaits();
+            return false;
+        }
+        if (!m_park.first_logged) {
+            m_park.first_logged = true;
+            LOG_INFO(Service,
+                     "EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT=1: frame fence waits park the nx2:vi2 service "
+                     "thread in the guest kernel (Lichii #1639; first at present #{})",
+                     m_presents);
+        }
+        auto& syncpoints = system.Host1x().GetSyncpointManager();
+        m_park_event->Clear(kernel);
+        m_park_signal_ns.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);
+        // Runs on the GPU thread as it reaches the fence, or inline here if it already did.
+        m_park_action.emplace(syncpoints, fence_id, fence_value,
+                              [event = m_park_event, kernel_ptr = &kernel,
+                               signal_ns = &m_park_signal_ns] {
+                                  signal_ns->store(std::chrono::steady_clock::now()
+                                                       .time_since_epoch()
+                                                       .count(),
+                                                   std::memory_order_relaxed);
+                                  event->Signal(*kernel_ptr);
+                              });
+        Kernel::KSynchronizationObject* object = &m_park_event->GetReadableEvent();
+        while (!syncpoints.IsReadyGuest(fence_id, fence_value) && system.IsPoweredOn() &&
+               std::chrono::steady_clock::now() < deadline) {
+            s32 index = -1;
+            // 50 ms rounds keep the power-off and deadline checks the host wait has.
+            const s64 timeout = kernel.HardwareTimer().GetTick() + 50'000'000;
+            const Result result =
+                Kernel::KSynchronizationObject::Wait(kernel, &index, &object, 1, timeout);
+            ++m_park.kernel_waits;
+            if (result == Kernel::ResultTimedOut) {
+                ++m_park.timeouts;
+            } else if (result == Kernel::ResultTerminationRequested) {
+                ++m_park.terminated;
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+        // Join an action still signalling before the next wait clears the event.
+        m_park_action.reset();
+        const auto end = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
+        const s64 signalled = m_park_signal_ns.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
+        if (signalled != 0) {
+            // From the fence action's signal to this thread running again (guest reschedule).
+            const s64 wake_us =
+                (end.time_since_epoch().count() - signalled) / 1000;
+            m_park.wake_us += wake_us > 0 ? static_cast<u64>(wake_us) : 0;
+            m_park.wake_max_us = (std::max)(m_park.wake_max_us,
+                                            wake_us > 0 ? static_cast<u64>(wake_us) : 0);
+            ++m_park.woken;
+        }
+        const auto waited_us =
+            std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(end - start).count();
+        m_park.parked_us += waited_us > 0 ? static_cast<u64>(waited_us) : 0;
+        ++m_park.parked;
+        NoteParkWaits();
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    /// EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT: one line per 600 waits that reached ParkFrameWait.
+    void NoteParkWaits() {
+        if (++m_park.waits < 600) {
+            return;
+        }
+        LOG_INFO(Service,
+                 "EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT: last {} frame waits (to present #{}): {} parked "
+                 "(avg {} us, {} kernel waits, {} 50-ms timeouts, {} ended by termination), "
+                 "signal-to-run avg {} us max {} us over {}; host wait kept: {} not on a guest "
+                 "thread, {} without an event",
+                 m_park.waits, m_presents, m_park.parked,
+                 m_park.parked != 0 ? m_park.parked_us / m_park.parked : 0, m_park.kernel_waits,
+                 m_park.timeouts, m_park.terminated,
+                 m_park.woken != 0 ? m_park.wake_us / m_park.woken : 0, m_park.wake_max_us,
+                 m_park.woken, m_park.host_thread, m_park.no_event);
+        const bool first_logged = m_park.first_logged;
+        m_park = {};
+        m_park.first_logged = first_logged;
+    }
+
+    struct ParkWaitCounts {
+        u64 waits{};
+        u64 parked{};
+        u64 parked_us{};
+        u64 kernel_waits{};
+        u64 timeouts{};
+        u64 terminated{};
+        u64 woken{};
+        u64 wake_us{};
+        u64 wake_max_us{};
+        u64 host_thread{};
+        u64 no_event{};
+        bool first_logged{};
+    };
+    ParkWaitCounts m_park{};
+    Kernel::KEvent* m_park_event{};
+    bool m_park_event_tried{};
+    std::atomic<s64> m_park_signal_ns{0};
+    std::optional<Nx2ScopedGuestFenceAction> m_park_action;
+
     /// Waits until the fence is reached. With `unless_gpu_waits`, gives up after a few ms once a
     /// GPU channel was seen suspended on a semaphore (it may be one this thread releases later).
     /// Returns whether the fence was reached.
@@ -2808,7 +2983,12 @@ private:
         if (progress_wait) [[unlikely]] {
             ReportWaitProgress(start);
         }
-        if (!syncpoints.IsReadyGuest(fence_id, fence_value)) {
+        // EDEN_UP_L1639_PARK_WAIT=1: a full wait (not the probe, not with
+        // EDEN_UP_FRAME_WAIT_PROGRESS) parks this service thread in the guest kernel instead of
+        // blocking the host thread of its guest core (ParkFrameWait); false = the host wait below.
+        if (!syncpoints.IsReadyGuest(fence_id, fence_value) &&
+            !(ParkWaitSwitch() && !unless_gpu_waits && !progress_wait &&
+              ParkFrameWait(fence_id, fence_value, start, deadline))) {
             // Woken by the GPU thread as it reaches the fence. Polling with 200 us sleeps slept
             // about 1 ms per check on Windows, added to every frame. An action left registered
             // after a timeout fires later on a state nobody waits on.
